Optionally let a stroke continue without force by the summed tip amplitudes (ZeroForceStrokeSum)

A pen that is pressed lightly reports force on and off within one stroke: its force tone falls
silent for some frames while the tip stays on the glass. The contact then has to continue on the
strength of the tip signal alone, and ZeroForceStrokeLevel ends it when that strength falls below a
fraction of what it was while the stroke had force.

The strength used so far is the energy of the interpolated peak, which is meant not to depend on
where the tip is between two antennas. With a tilted pen it depends on it more than anything else.
Measured on the Surface Pro 9 with the Slim Pen 2 on a capture of 9 glyphs written without lifting
the pen, light pressure, 46 degrees of tilt:

- the force tone was absent 6 times within a glyph, for 4 to 24 windows (30 to 180 ms);
- within a stroke the energy of the interpolated peak changes between 1.0 and 3.2 times its
  reference (the antenna on the side the pen leans to has almost no signal, the parabola through
  three antennas overshoots), so it fell below 0.85 of its average over the stroke in 3 of the 6
  intervals although nothing had changed: 12 strokes instead of 9, with gaps of 21, 67 and 67 ms;
- the sum of the amplitudes of the three antennas around the strongest one, squared and added for
  both axes, stays within 0.81 .. 1.02 of its value before the interval in all 6 (as an amplitude
  its relative standard deviation within a stroke is 2 .. 3 % at any tilt, where the interpolated
  peak has 2.3 % with an upright pen and 9.5 % and more with a tilted one).

With ZeroForceStrokeSum a stroke that had force continues without force for as long as that sum
stays above ZeroForceStrokeLevel times its average during the force, and neither the interpolated
peak nor ZeroForceExit is consulted for it. How a contact without any force begins
(ZeroForceEnter, ZeroForceStartSamples), how one that never had force ends (ZeroForceExit), the
reference (ZeroForceReference) and the lockout after a firm stroke are unchanged.

Where the pen is really lifted the sum falls below 0.9 of its value in the third to fourth window
after the force ended (median over 171 lifts of four captures), as before.

With ZeroForceStrokeSum and ZeroForceStrokeLevel = 0.8 (0.85 with the interpolated peak before):

- the 9 glyphs: 9 strokes (12 before);
- handwriting captures, strokes per phase before -> after: small light writing 49 -> 45, normal
  32 -> 30 and 31 -> 31, tilted 20 -> 18, light 20 -> 19, a short text 44 -> 44; ticks, dots and
  hatches (15, 24, 10) unchanged; nothing while hovering, a firm stroke lifted quickly ends at
  the same sample;
- the strokes that are now joined are short intervals without force in which the summed amplitude
  stayed above 0.9 of its value before: by the tip signal the tip did not leave the glass, and
  such an interval can not be told from the ones inside the 9 glyphs. With 0.85 the 9 glyphs give
  10 strokes and the tilted writing 24.

Off by default.

